Apps built with dart_smb2 also bundle libsmb2, which the package's build
hook downloads for each target:

  libsmb2 - SMB2/3 client library
  Copyright (C) Ronnie Sahlberg and the libsmb2 contributors
  https://github.com/sahlberg/libsmb2
  License: GNU Lesser General Public License, version 2.1 or later

From libsmb2-r11 each binary also contains, linked statically, the
libcrypto of OpenSSL, which signs and encrypts the SMB3 traffic:

  OpenSSL - cryptography library
  Copyright (c) 1998-2026 The OpenSSL Project Authors
  https://www.openssl.org
  License: Apache License, version 2.0

The binaries are built by libsmb2-scripts
(https://github.com/ales-drnz/libsmb2-scripts) and published on the
libsmb2-rN releases of https://github.com/ales-drnz/dart_smb2. Each
release carries the license texts (COPYING, LICENCE-LGPL-2.1.txt and,
from r11, LICENSE-OpenSSL.txt) and names the libsmb2-scripts commit it
was built from, which is the corresponding source of its binaries.

An app that distributes libsmb2 this way should include the LGPL-2.1
text, OpenSSL's Apache-2.0 license and this notice, and point to that
source. Flutter's license page does not list libsmb2 or OpenSSL by
itself: an app that shows one adds them, for example with
LicenseRegistry.addLicense and the texts of the release. The library is
loaded dynamically, and hooks.user_defines.dart_smb2.libsmb2 replaces it
with another build (see the package README).
